    "problem_statement": "Fragment.remove() method returns None instead of self\n\n#### Description\n\nThe Fragment.remove() method is not returning self as expected, breaking method chaining. When calling remove() on a Fragment instance, it returns None instead of the Fragment object.\n\n#### Steps/Code to Reproduce\n\n```python\nimport furl\n\nf = furl.Fragment('a/path/great/job?lol=sup&foo=blorp')\nresult = f.remove(path='job', args=['lol'])\nprint(result)  # Expected: Fragment object, Actual: None\n```\n\nThe remove() method should return the Fragment instance to allow method chaining like:\n\n```python\nf = furl.Fragment('a/path/great/job?lol=sup&foo=blorp')\nf.remove(path='job', args=['lol']).remove(path=['path', 'great'], args=['foo'])\n```\n\nBut currently this fails because the first remove() call returns None."
